--TEST--
A `Core` enum is an ordinary enum at every site a declared one reaches: a binding, `==`, `as int`, a conversion back, a `match`, an `array<T>` element and a member's argument — `rule:enums/closed-integer-type`, `rule:enums/no-class-machinery`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/core-enums
// `rule:enums/closed-integer-type`: the enums the library ships are declared the same way a
// program's own are, so nothing here is a site a declared enum could not reach.
Core\Order $dir = Core\Order::Desc;
echo $dir as int, "\n";
echo $dir == Core\Order::Asc ? "asc" : "desc", "\n";

// A number converts back into a case, and one no case names is `null` under the
// nullable spelling rather than a case picked by default.
Core\Order $back = 0 as Core\Order;
echo $back == Core\Order::Asc ? "asc" : "desc", "\n";
?Core\Order $missing = 9 as ?Core\Order;
echo $missing == null ? "null" : "some", "\n";

// An element type and a `match` subject, the match exhaustive over the two
// cases the library declares rather than over a subset named here.
array<Core\Order> $both = [Core\Order::Asc, Core\Order::Desc];
foreach ($both as Core\Order $o) {
    echo match ($o) {
        Core\Order::Asc => "up",
        Core\Order::Desc => "down",
    }, "\n";
}

// The argument site the enum exists for, reached from a binding rather than
// from a case written at the call.
array<int> $xs = [3, 1, 2];
foreach (Core\Arr::sort($xs, {order: $dir}) as int $v) {
    echo $v;
}
echo "\n";

// A second `Core` enum, to pin that the two are told apart by their own
// declarations rather than by the ordinals they share.
echo Core\RoundMode::HalfUp as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--
1
desc
asc
null
up
down
321
0
